{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Warren1968-T1-15L-m2",
  "Source": "S. W. Warren, \"Properties and performance of basic designs of infrared interference filters,\" Infrared Phys. 8, 65-78 (1968). DOI not printed in the paper - Table 1, p. 66 (design HLHLHLHmLLHLHLHLHS with m = 2); notation defined p. 66",
  "Notes": "Single-cavity (Fabry-Perot type) all-dielectric bandpass filter, 15 layers, low-index spacer of order m = 2 (second-order, 4 QWOT)\nSpecification: Bandpass centred at lambda0 (lambda0/lambda = 1); bandwidth tabulated as % of peak wavelength.\nReported: Table 1 (p. 66): FWHM bandwidth 0.057 % of peak wavelength (spacer order m = 2).\nNotes: Design string written out: HLHLHLHLLLLHLHLHLHS. Spacer mLL = LLLL = 4 QWOT of L. H = quarter-wave optical thickness of index 4.0, L = quarter wave of index 1.8 at lambda0, S = substrate of index 1.65 (p. 66); adjacent equal letters (HH, LL) are merged into one half-wave layer of 2 QWOT. The paper writes designs from the incident (air) side to S, so the order is kept as printed. lambda0 is not fixed for Tables 1-3 and the figures (results are in lambda0/lambda); 4000 nm (4 um, as in Tables 4-6) is used, which is harmless because all indices are constant. Recomputations against the paper match only if the uncoated back surface of the n=1.65 substrate is included incoherently (substrate -> air): air/L/1.65 alone gives T = 89.4 % at lambda0 while Table 4 prints 84.6 %, and with the back surface it is 84.6 %; the rejection minima of Sec. 2.1.3 (3.45 %, 0.087 %, 0.0020 %, 0.000047 %) also match exactly only with the back surface. Normal incidence, unpolarised. Bandwidth = full width at half peak transmission, in % of peak wavelength. A check with the back surface included gave 5.49 % (paper 5.26) for HLHHLHS, 2.30 % (2.20) for HLHLLHLHS and 1.00 % (0.97) for HLHLHHLHLHS, i.e. about 4 % larger than printed; the 1968 program's definition or sampling may differ slightly.\nLayers from Table 1, p. 66 (design HLHLHLHmLLHLHLHLHS with m = 2); notation defined p. 66; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours FWHM 0.0577 % of 4 um (peak T 88.7 %); paper FWHM 0.057 % (Table 1).",
